On Mon, 16 Aug 2004 22:54:47 +0800, Nathan Maloney <fake@email.com> wrote:
> (these things I've noticed from my own playing, if it's inaccurate, I  
> apologise)

> Any sort of fresh, edible meat will tame a dog or cat. Corpses that they  
> like (ie not lichen or acid blobs) will tame 'em straight away. Don't try  
> to throw tins :P

Throwing tins makes them peaceful. They know there is food in them, even
though they can never eat them.

> Lichen, fungi, fruit and vegetables will tame horses.

> Throwing horse food at dogs and cats will make them peaceful, the same for  
> throwing meat at horses.

If the monster is tamed, the food is used up; if it is made peaceful,
the food remains ("<food> misses the <monster>").

> For taming dogs and cats, I prefer the last available monster corpse,  
> otherwise a precious food ration. Tripe works wonders for training an  
> already tame one, and it's a great find early on.

I prefer fortune cookies for taming. Light and very little nutrition
"wasted".

> As for dragons and other exotic pets, wand/spell/trap of polymorphing an  
> already tame pet works (trap is favourite because it doesn't hurt them),  
> otherwise magical taming as already mentioned. Note that it's harder the  
> higher level the monster is.

A more important factor is the MR% of the monster, which together with
the level of the attack and monster determines whether certain effects
(like taming and polymorph) are resisted. For example, dragons,
disenchanters and minotaurs are easily tamed, even though they are high
level. Most spellcasting monsters have very high MR%. The attack level
is your experience level if it is a spell and a fixed value depending on
the device used otherwise (this fixed value is never more than 12).

